WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.878
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.878 --> 00:00:10.590
The US system is designed to do nothing.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.830
So that means to get something passed and

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.100
to become a law you need a majority in the
house.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.950
You need a super majority in the senate,
and

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.200
you also need the President to sign it.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.670
Right?
And that's aside from

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.910
other institutional constraints, like the
committee system, and so on, right?

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.150
So when the Democrats hold the majority,
and the Democrats hold the White House,

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.280
the US system is gonna restrain their
agenda, right?

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.806
That's just the way the system is, that's
the way the founders intended.

00:00:35.806 --> 00:00:38.651
[MUSIC]

00:00:38.651 --> 00:00:41.900
What we do in this study, is to try to ask
the question, why?

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.780
Why has the institution evolved to look
the way it does?

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.370
And so what we do is we examine two much
maligned aspects of the U.S.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.170
political institution,

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:55.960
they are supermajority role and the
delegation of authority to bureaucrats.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.695
Now both of these can be seen as
undemocratic.

00:00:59.695 --> 00:01:02.840
The filibuster rule and that it's not
respecting majority rule,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.190
it's not representing the people as well
as it should.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.996
And the delegation to bureaucrats who are
unelected, all right?

00:01:08.996 --> 00:01:12.104
So it's often asked why is authority
delegated to these sorts of

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:14.181
people who aren't elected by the people?

00:01:14.181 --> 00:01:17.396
[MUSIC]

00:01:17.396 --> 00:01:18.629
What we find is that,

00:01:18.629 --> 00:01:23.284
these two components, the super majority
filibuster rule and the delegation of

00:01:23.284 --> 00:01:28.920
authority to unelected bureaucrats, on
their own have seemed very undemocratic.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.753
Put together, all right?

00:01:29.753 --> 00:01:33.663
They work in such a way that the
interdependence actually leads to

00:01:33.663 --> 00:01:37.159
better policy outcomes than we otherwise
would achieve.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:40.548
[MUSIC]

00:01:40.548 --> 00:01:43.898
What most analysis or most commentary on
the gridlock misses is

00:01:43.898 --> 00:01:47.070
that there's a lot of action happening in
the background.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.450
And what we're trying to point out is a
lot of this action

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.680
is happening via delegation.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.640
So Congress may fight.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.570
They may be able to, may not be able to
agree about whether the tax rate should be

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:00.540
a little higher or a little lower.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.430
But what they can agree upon, in a world
that's changing over time,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.430
is that they want policy to be smoothed
out in a sense,

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:14.250
to not change too much as the world
changes, to react to the changing world.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:14.750
All right?

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.730
Because one thing that politicians like,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.710
particularly incumbent politicians is,
they like the status quo.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:21.990
All right?

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:26.060
They like the world to not change because
that world is what got them elected.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.760
And so, as much as they can, they'd like
to keep the world the way it is.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.390
Gridlock serves that purpose.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:35.110
And so, to that extent, it's kind of
locking down the world or

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.020
locking down some policies for the
interests of these policymakers.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.400
That allows deals to be made in Congress.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.105
And what we're trying to show in this
research is that delegation is

00:02:45.105 --> 00:02:49.330
a way that Congress can overcome the
problems of gridlock and get deals done.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.511
[MUSIC]

00:02:52.511 --> 00:02:56.379
So to take a recent example, we can look
at the Dodd-Frank Bill where a lots of

00:02:56.379 --> 00:02:59.730
authority was reg, delegated to the
financial regulators.

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:03.150
Now, that may have been a puzzle.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.420
Why didn't Congress think there's nothing
more [UNKNOWN] or

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:10.040
nothing more important in the last five
years than financial reform?

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.450
Why didn't Congress figure out the answer
themselves?

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.900
What they did was, they delegated
authority to the regulators,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.780
because they didn't know how the world was
going to change.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.940
What Congress people could agree upon is
that they wanted to find a solution.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.240
And I didn't know what it was, and I
didn't know what it would be tomorrow, but

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.630
the deal they struck was to delegate that
authority to a regulator,

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.890
who had different preferences to them.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.400
So it may seem undemocratic, but

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.320
it's those different preferences that
allow the deal to happen.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.810
Yesterday, Democrats and Republicans
agreed something needed to be done.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.530
I didn't know what it was, but

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.390
they could agree to delegate authority to
that agency.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.435
Tomorrow, when the agency starts figuring
out what the answer is, Republicans or

00:03:50.435 --> 00:03:52.226
maybe Democrats don't like it, but

00:03:52.226 --> 00:03:56.358
they can't back track, they can't roll it
back, precisely because of gridlock.

00:03:56.358 --> 00:03:59.470
[MUSIC]

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.430
Gridlock has its pluses and has its
minuses.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:02.710
All right?

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.210
It does stop things from happening and it
frustrates a lot of people.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.240
What we're trying to show here is the
gridlock may not be as bad as it,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.750
bad as it seems.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.306
And that other aspects of the political
institution go a long way to

00:04:14.306 --> 00:04:17.874
solving the problems or solving the
inefficiencies of gridlock.

00:04:17.874 --> 00:04:27.739
[MUSIC]

